1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,707
This is MATH 239, video 6. The topic is the Binomial Theorem and the binomial series, section 2.1 of the notes.

2
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:19,223
After this video, you can prove two formulas: one for 1 plus x to the n, and one for 1 over 1 minus x to the t.

3
00:00:19,673 --> 00:00:24,580
You can use the Binomial Theorem to prove the Vandermonde Convolution.

4
00:00:25,030 --> 00:00:29,211
You can find a coefficient of a product of binomial series.

5
00:00:29,661 --> 00:00:39,069
We solve Exercises 2.1 and 2.2 of the notes, Tutorial 2 question 2, and Practice Midterm question 7.

6
00:00:39,519 --> 00:00:47,327
Chapter 2 calculates with power series. A power series G of x is the sum of g n times x to the n.

7
00:00:47,777 --> 00:00:56,353
The coefficient of x to the n in G of x is written with square brackets: x to the n, then G of x. It is g n.

8
00:00:56,803 --> 00:01:00,089
x is an indeterminate. It has no value.

9
00:01:00,539 --> 00:01:09,349
The first example: every coefficient is 1. Draw G as a row of cells. Cell n holds the coefficient of x to the n.

10
00:01:09,799 --> 00:01:16,839
Multiply G by x. Every term moves up one power. So each 1 moves one cell to the right.

11
00:01:17,289 --> 00:01:19,764
The first cell of x G becomes 0.

12
00:01:20,214 --> 00:01:26,529
Subtract x G from G. In every cell after the first, 1 minus 1 is 0.

13
00:01:26,979 --> 00:01:35,491
In the first cell, 1 minus 0 is 1. Only the constant 1 stays. So 1 minus x, times G, equals 1.

14
00:01:35,941 --> 00:01:44,218
This is the geometric series. 1 over 1 minus x is the series 1 plus x plus x squared, and so on.

15
00:01:44,668 --> 00:01:50,513
We never put a number in for x, so we do not need convergence. This is algebra.

16
00:01:50,963 --> 00:02:04,403
Theorem 2.2 is the Binomial Theorem. For each natural number n, 1 plus x to the n equals the sum over k from 0 to n of n choose k, times x to the k.

17
00:02:04,853 --> 00:02:09,333
First, look at n equals 3: three factors of 1 plus x.

18
00:02:09,783 --> 00:02:20,941
To multiply out, take one term from each factor, and multiply. This row takes x from factor 1, and 1 from factors 2 and 3. The product is x.

19
00:02:21,391 --> 00:02:31,225
Each factor gives 2 choices. So there are 2 times 2 times 2, which is 8, products. The orange cells show where each product takes x.

20
00:02:31,675 --> 00:02:35,814
Group the products by the power of x. One product is 1.

21
00:02:36,264 --> 00:02:38,013
Three products are x.

22
00:02:38,463 --> 00:02:40,511
Three products are x squared.

23
00:02:40,961 --> 00:02:42,924
One product is x cubed.

24
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:49,625
So 1 plus x, all cubed, is 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed.

25
00:02:50,075 --> 00:03:03,749
The number of products equal to x to the k is the number of ways to pick the k factors that give x. That is 3 choose k. The notes prove the general case with a bijection, on the next two slides.

26
00:03:04,199 --> 00:03:07,655
P of n is the set of all subsets of 1 to n.

27
00:03:08,105 --> 00:03:16,425
Curly 0 1 to the n is the set of indicator vectors: vectors of length n with entries 0 or 1.

28
00:03:16,875 --> 00:03:27,265
Example 1.12 gives a bijection between the two sets. Take n equals 8, and the subset S equal to 2, 3, 5, 7.

29
00:03:27,715 --> 00:03:35,800
Its indicator vector alpha has a 1 in positions 2, 3, 5 and 7, and a 0 in the other positions.

30
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:43,013
The inverse map collects the positions of the 1s. So the two maps are mutually inverse bijections.

31
00:03:43,463 --> 00:03:50,076
The entries of alpha add up to the size of S. Here there are 4 ones, and S has 4 elements.

32
00:03:50,526 --> 00:03:56,563
In general, S matches alpha, and the size of S is a 1 plus a 2, up to a n.

33
00:03:57,013 --> 00:04:03,861
Put x to the size of S on each subset. The table shows all 8 subsets for n equals 3.

34
00:04:04,311 --> 00:04:15,298
Because of the bijection, the sum of x to the size of S over all subsets equals the sum of x to the a 1 plus up to a n, over all indicator vectors.

35
00:04:15,748 --> 00:04:21,999
The last column of the table adds to 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed.

36
00:04:22,449 --> 00:04:28,529
This is 1 plus x, all cubed, again. Now we simplify the two sums in general.

37
00:04:28,979 --> 00:04:35,059
Start from the equation of the last slide. The left side, LHS, sums over subsets.

38
00:04:35,509 --> 00:04:39,413
The right side, RHS, sums over indicator vectors.

39
00:04:39,863 --> 00:04:48,588
Left side. Group the subsets by their size k. By Theorem 1.5, there are n choose k subsets of size k.

40
00:04:49,038 --> 00:04:56,611
Each subset of size k gives x to the k. So the left side is the sum of n choose k, x to the k.

41
00:04:57,061 --> 00:05:12,656
Right side. A sum over all vectors is a sum over a 1 in 0, 1, then a 2 in 0, 1, and so on. Also, x to the a 1 plus up to a n is x to the a 1, times x to the a 2, and so on.

42
00:05:13,106 --> 00:05:16,989
So the nested sums split into a product of n separate sums.

43
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:24,671
Here is why the split is correct, for n equals 2. Each cell of the grid is one vector a 1, a 2.

44
00:05:25,121 --> 00:05:34,294
The entry of a cell is x to the a 1, times x to the a 2. For example, cell 1, 1 is x times x, which is x squared.

45
00:05:34,744 --> 00:05:45,069
The sum of the 4 cells is 1 plus 2 x plus x squared. This is the row sum, 1 plus x, times the column sum, 1 plus x.

46
00:05:45,519 --> 00:05:50,917
Each separate sum is x to the 0 plus x to the 1, which is 1 plus x.

47
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:57,959
There are n of them, so the right side is 1 plus x to the n. This proves the Binomial Theorem.

48
00:05:58,409 --> 00:06:13,961
Example 2.3 uses the Binomial Theorem to prove the Vandermonde Convolution. Start with an identity: 1 plus x to the m plus n equals 1 plus x to the m, times 1 plus x to the n.

49
00:06:14,411 --> 00:06:22,475
The grid shows m equals 3 and n equals 4. Each row is a term of the first factor, 1 plus x, cubed.

50
00:06:22,925 --> 00:06:27,597
Each column is a term of the second factor, 1 plus x, to the 4.

51
00:06:28,047 --> 00:06:38,031
A cell is the product of its row term and its column term. For example, row 3 x times column 6 x squared is 18 x cubed.

52
00:06:38,481 --> 00:06:47,377
In general, the cell in row j and column i is m choose j, times n choose i, times x to the j plus i.

53
00:06:47,827 --> 00:06:55,784
All cells with the same j plus i are on one diagonal. The purple diagonal holds the x cubed terms.

54
00:06:56,234 --> 00:07:05,706
On the right side, the coefficient of x cubed is the sum of the diagonal: 4 plus 18 plus 12 plus 1, which is 35.

55
00:07:06,156 --> 00:07:10,636
On the left side, it is 7 choose 3, which is also 35.

56
00:07:11,086 --> 00:07:23,865
In general, re-index the double sum by k equals j plus i. Two equal polynomials have equal coefficients, so compare the coefficient of x to the k on both sides.

57
00:07:24,315 --> 00:07:35,024
So m plus n choose k equals the sum over j of m choose j, times n choose k minus j. This is the Vandermonde Convolution.

58
00:07:35,474 --> 00:07:46,354
Now a series with infinitely many terms. For t equals 1, 1 over 1 minus x is the geometric series. Every coefficient is 1.

59
00:07:46,804 --> 00:07:54,207
Recall multisets from Chapter 1. M of t is the set of all multisets with t types of elements.

60
00:07:54,657 --> 00:08:03,553
A multiset is a multiplicity vector m 1 to m t of natural numbers. Its size is m 1 plus up to m t.

61
00:08:04,003 --> 00:08:12,131
By Theorem 1.9, the number of multisets of size n is n plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1.

62
00:08:12,581 --> 00:08:20,367
For t equals 2 this is n plus 1 choose 1, which is n plus 1: 1, 2, 3, 4, and so on.

63
00:08:20,817 --> 00:08:27,580
For t equals 3 it is n plus 2 choose 2: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, and so on.

64
00:08:28,030 --> 00:08:37,161
The orange cell: there are 4 plus 2 choose 2, which is 6 choose 2, which is 15, multisets of size 4 with 3 types.

65
00:08:37,611 --> 00:08:53,525
Theorem 2.4 is the binomial series. For each positive integer t, 1 over 1 minus x to the t equals the sum over n of n plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1, times x to the n.

66
00:08:53,975 --> 00:08:59,223
So the row for t types is the series 1 over 1 minus x to the t.

67
00:08:59,673 --> 00:09:03,663
The row for t equals 4 starts 1, 4, 10, 20.

68
00:09:04,113 --> 00:09:14,097
The notes call this the binomial series with a negative integer exponent. The general binomial series comes in Section 4.4.1.

69
00:09:14,547 --> 00:09:21,288
Also, n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1 equals n plus t minus 1 choose n.

70
00:09:21,738 --> 00:09:33,066
The proof follows the same plan as the Binomial Theorem. A multiset with t types is a vector of t natural numbers. So M of t equals N to the t.

71
00:09:33,516 --> 00:09:40,492
By Theorem 1.9, the left sum collects x to the size of mu, over all multisets mu.

72
00:09:40,942 --> 00:09:52,739
A sum over N to the t is t nested sums, one for each m i, from 0 to infinity. And x to the size of mu is the product of the x to the m i.

73
00:09:53,189 --> 00:10:03,664
The grid shows t equals 2. Cell m 1, m 2 holds x to the m 1 plus m 2. For example, cell 1, 2 holds x cubed.

74
00:10:04,114 --> 00:10:09,426
The purple cells have m 1 plus m 2 equal to 4. There are 5 of them.

75
00:10:09,876 --> 00:10:17,769
So the coefficient of x to the 4 is 5. The formula gives 4 plus 1 choose 1, which is also 5.

76
00:10:18,219 --> 00:10:21,825
The nested sums split into a product of t equal sums.

77
00:10:22,275 --> 00:10:33,155
Each sum is the geometric series, 1 over 1 minus x. So the result is 1 over 1 minus x to the t. This proves Theorem 2.4.

78
00:10:33,605 --> 00:10:46,405
Exam questions use these formulas with a small change. Here is 1 over 1 minus y, the geometric series in a new indeterminate y. Every coefficient is 1.

79
00:10:46,855 --> 00:10:49,436
Now replace y by 3 x squared.

80
00:10:49,886 --> 00:10:56,883
The term y to the i becomes 3 x squared to the i, which is 3 to the i times x to the 2 i.

81
00:10:57,333 --> 00:11:03,243
So the cell of y to the i moves to x to the 2 i, and gets the factor 3 to the i.

82
00:11:03,693 --> 00:11:11,351
For example, y squared moves to x to the 4. It gets the factor 3 squared, so the coefficient is 9.

83
00:11:11,801 --> 00:11:13,935
The odd powers of x get 0.

84
00:11:14,385 --> 00:11:30,918
The general rule. Replace x by c x to the m in Theorem 2.4. The coefficient of x to the m i is i plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1, times c to the i. Other powers have coefficient 0.

85
00:11:31,368 --> 00:11:40,136
In the Binomial Theorem, replace x by c x: the coefficient of x to the k is n choose k, times c to the k.

86
00:11:40,586 --> 00:11:46,111
The shift rule. Multiplying by x to the a moves every cell a places to the right.

87
00:11:46,561 --> 00:11:54,881
So the coefficient of x to the n in x to the a times F equals the coefficient of x to the n minus a in F.

88
00:11:55,331 --> 00:12:09,305
Now some exercises from the notes. Exercise 2.1 asks for coefficients. Part a: the coefficient of x to the 8 in 1 minus x to the minus 7. Pause the video and try it.

89
00:12:09,755 --> 00:12:17,499
This is Theorem 2.4 with t equals 7. The coefficient of x to the n is n plus 6 choose 6.

90
00:12:17,949 --> 00:12:24,775
With n equals 8, we get 14 choose 6, which is 3003. The green cell shows it.

91
00:12:25,225 --> 00:12:34,804
Part b: the coefficient of x to the 10 in x to the 6, times 1 minus 2 x to the minus 5. Pause the video and try it.

92
00:12:35,254 --> 00:12:50,401
First expand 1 minus 2 x to the minus 5. Replace x by 2 x in Theorem 2.4 with t equals 5. The coefficient of x to the i is i plus 4 choose 4, times 2 to the i.

93
00:12:50,851 --> 00:12:56,504
The row starts 1, 10, 60, 280, 1120.

94
00:12:56,954 --> 00:13:01,754
Multiplying by x to the 6 moves the row 6 places to the right.

95
00:13:02,204 --> 00:13:11,676
So the coefficient of x to the 10 is the coefficient of x to the 10 minus 6, which is x to the 4, in the row before the shift.

96
00:13:12,126 --> 00:13:20,083
That is 8 choose 4 times 2 to the 4, which is 70 times 16, or 1120.

97
00:13:20,533 --> 00:13:31,179
Part c: the coefficient of x to the 8 in x cubed plus 5 x to the 4, times 1 plus 3 x to the 6. Pause the video and try it.

98
00:13:31,629 --> 00:13:43,106
Let F be 1 plus 3 x to the 6. By the Binomial Theorem with x replaced by 3 x, its coefficients are 6 choose k, times 3 to the k.

99
00:13:43,556 --> 00:13:50,148
Split the product into two terms. In x cubed times F, the row of F moves 3 places.

100
00:13:50,598 --> 00:13:58,065
In 5 x to the 4 times F, the row moves 4 places, and every cell is multiplied by 5.

101
00:13:58,515 --> 00:14:10,397
We need the column of x to the 8. By the shift rule, it holds the coefficient of x to the 5 in F, plus 5 times the coefficient of x to the 4 in F.

102
00:14:10,847 --> 00:14:22,922
The coefficient of x to the 5 in F is 6 choose 5 times 3 to the 5. That is 6 times 243, which is 1458.

103
00:14:23,372 --> 00:14:36,855
5 times the coefficient of x to the 4 in F is 5 times 6 choose 4 times 3 to the 4. That is 5 times 15 times 81, which is 6075.

104
00:14:37,305 --> 00:14:47,203
The sum of the orange column is 1458 plus 6075, which is 7533.

105
00:14:47,653 --> 00:14:58,277
Part d: the coefficient of x to the 9 in 1 minus 4 x to the 5, plus 1 minus 3 x to the minus 2. Pause the video and try it.

106
00:14:58,727 --> 00:15:06,173
The coefficient of a sum is the sum of the coefficients. Look at the first term, 1 minus 4 x to the 5.

107
00:15:06,623 --> 00:15:17,204
It has a positive exponent. By the Binomial Theorem, it is a polynomial. Its coefficients are 5 choose k, times minus 4 to the k.

108
00:15:17,654 --> 00:15:23,926
A polynomial of degree 5 has no x to the 9 term. So the first part gives 0.

109
00:15:24,376 --> 00:15:29,112
Do not use the binomial series formula for a positive exponent.

110
00:15:29,562 --> 00:15:35,749
The second term is Theorem 2.4 with t equals 2, and x replaced by 3 x.

111
00:15:36,199 --> 00:15:41,873
The coefficient of x to the 9 is 9 plus 1 choose 1, times 3 to the 9.

112
00:15:42,323 --> 00:15:55,422
3 to the 9 is 19,683. So the answer is 10 times 19,683, which is 196,830.

113
00:15:55,872 --> 00:16:09,376
Tutorial 2, question 2. Find the coefficient of x to the 11 in x squared over 1 minus x cubed to the 5, times 1 minus 3 x squared. Pause the video and try it.

114
00:16:09,826 --> 00:16:23,522
Step 1, the shift. The factor x squared moves everything 2 places. So we need the coefficient of x to the 11 minus 2, which is x to the 9, in the product of the two other factors.

115
00:16:23,972 --> 00:16:33,743
Step 2, expand each factor with Theorem 2.4. The first factor has terms i plus 4 choose 4, times x to the 3 i.

116
00:16:34,193 --> 00:16:38,801
The second factor has terms 3 to the j, times x to the 2 j.

117
00:16:39,251 --> 00:16:49,320
Step 3. A product of one term from each factor has exponent 3 i plus 2 j. The grid shows this exponent for each pair i, j.

118
00:16:49,770 --> 00:16:52,394
The rows are the terms of the first factor.

119
00:16:52,844 --> 00:16:55,831
The columns are the terms of the second factor.

120
00:16:56,281 --> 00:17:05,987
We need exponent 9. Two cells are green: i equals 1 with j equals 3, and i equals 3 with j equals 0.

121
00:17:06,437 --> 00:17:14,715
The red cells are larger than 9. Larger i or j only makes the exponent larger, so no other pair works.

122
00:17:15,165 --> 00:17:27,218
Step 4, add the products. i equals 1, j equals 3 gives 5 choose 4, times 3 cubed. That is 5 times 27, which is 135.

123
00:17:27,668 --> 00:17:35,327
i equals 3, j equals 0 gives 7 choose 4, times 3 to the 0. That is 35.

124
00:17:35,777 --> 00:17:41,558
The coefficient is 135 plus 35, which is 170.

125
00:17:42,008 --> 00:17:58,413
Practice Midterm question 7. Find the exact value of the coefficient of x to the 70 in x cubed, over 1 minus 4 x to the 30, to the 20, times 1 minus x, all cubed. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:17:58,863 --> 00:18:08,079
Step 1, the shift. Remove x cubed. 70 minus 3 is 67, so we need the coefficient of x to the 67.

127
00:18:08,529 --> 00:18:25,809
Step 2, expand. The first factor is Theorem 2.4 with t equals 20, and x replaced by 4 x to the 30. Its terms are i plus 19 choose 19, times 4 to the i, times x to the 30 i.

128
00:18:26,259 --> 00:18:33,449
The second factor has t equals 3. Its terms are j plus 2 choose 2, times x to the j.

129
00:18:33,899 --> 00:18:46,421
Step 3. We need 30 i plus j equal to 67, with j at least 0. Each bar has length 67: an orange part 30 i, and a blue part j.

130
00:18:46,871 --> 00:18:54,423
For i equals 0, there is no orange part. j is 67 minus 0, which is 67.

131
00:18:54,873 --> 00:19:00,121
For i equals 1, j is 67 minus 30, which is 37.

132
00:19:00,571 --> 00:19:05,521
For i equals 2, j is 67 minus 60, which is 7.

133
00:19:05,971 --> 00:19:14,867
For i equals 3, 30 i is 90, which is more than 67. Then j is negative. So there are only three terms.

134
00:19:15,317 --> 00:19:27,413
Step 4, one term for each bar. For i equals 0, j plus 2 is 69. The term is 69 choose 2, which is 2346.

135
00:19:27,863 --> 00:19:44,737
For i equals 1, j plus 2 is 39. The term is 20 choose 19, times 4, times 39 choose 2. That is 20 times 4 times 741, which is 59,280.

136
00:19:45,187 --> 00:20:02,745
For i equals 2, j plus 2 is 9. The term is 21 choose 19, times 4 squared, times 9 choose 2. That is 210 times 16 times 36, which is 120,960.

137
00:20:03,195 --> 00:20:17,893
Add the three terms: 2346 plus 59,280 plus 120,960 is 182,586.

138
00:20:18,343 --> 00:20:22,738
The question says that you can stop at the sum of binomials and powers.

139
00:20:23,188 --> 00:20:40,212
Exercise 2.2 goes the other way. Part f gives the first coefficients: 1, 6, 24, 80, 240, 672. Find a binomial series that begins like this. Pause the video and try it.

140
00:20:40,662 --> 00:20:47,745
From the substitution rule, a coefficient has the form c to the n, times a binomial coefficient.

141
00:20:48,195 --> 00:21:00,760
The coefficients have many factors of 2, so try c equals 2. For example, 80 is 2 cubed times 10, and 672 is 2 to the 5 times 21.

142
00:21:01,210 --> 00:21:03,941
Divide each coefficient by 2 to the n.

143
00:21:04,391 --> 00:21:07,996
The result is 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21.

144
00:21:08,446 --> 00:21:14,697
This is the row for t equals 3 on the binomial series slide: n plus 2 choose 2.

145
00:21:15,147 --> 00:21:18,880
So the series is 1 over 1 minus 2 x, cubed.

146
00:21:19,330 --> 00:21:28,738
Part e: 1 minus 4 x squared plus 12 x to the 4 minus 32 x to the 6, and so on. Pause the video and try it.

147
00:21:29,188 --> 00:21:39,812
Only even powers appear, so the series has x squared in place of x: m equals 2. Column n holds the coefficient of x to the 2 n.

148
00:21:40,262 --> 00:21:45,147
The signs alternate, so c is negative. Try c equals minus 2.

149
00:21:45,597 --> 00:21:48,755
Divide each coefficient by minus 2 to the n.

150
00:21:49,205 --> 00:21:52,319
The result is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

151
00:21:52,769 --> 00:21:56,780
This is n plus 1 choose 1, the row for t equals 2.

152
00:21:57,230 --> 00:22:06,702
So the series is 1 over 1 minus minus 2 x squared, all squared. That is 1 over 1 plus 2 x squared, all squared.

153
00:22:07,152 --> 00:22:16,197
Here is the method for every question of this type. Step 1: pull out any power x to the a, and lower the target exponent by a.

154
00:22:16,647 --> 00:22:25,650
Step 2: expand each factor with the Binomial Theorem or the binomial series. Write the general term with its own index.

155
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:39,711
Step 3: write the exponent equation, for example 3 i plus 2 j equals 9. List every solution in natural numbers. Go through the values of the index with the largest step.

156
00:22:40,161 --> 00:22:45,345
Step 4: each solution gives one product of coefficients. Add the products.

157
00:22:45,795 --> 00:22:53,496
Common errors. A positive exponent gives a polynomial, so its coefficients are zero after x to the n.

158
00:22:53,946 --> 00:22:59,642
After the substitution of c x to the m, do not forget the factor c to the i.

159
00:23:00,092 --> 00:23:03,612
Do not miss a solution of the exponent equation.

160
00:23:04,062 --> 00:23:09,438
In a written answer, name the result you use, show the solutions, and show the sum.

161
00:23:09,888 --> 00:23:16,075
To recap. The geometric series comes from 1 minus x, times G, equals 1.

162
00:23:16,525 --> 00:23:22,306
The Binomial Theorem comes from the bijection between subsets and indicator vectors.

163
00:23:22,756 --> 00:23:28,111
The Vandermonde Convolution compares coefficients in a product of two binomials.

164
00:23:28,561 --> 00:23:37,094
The binomial series for 1 over 1 minus x to the t comes from multisets, because M of t equals N to the t.

165
00:23:37,544 --> 00:23:43,069
To find a coefficient, substitute c x to the m, and shift by x to the a.

166
00:23:43,519 --> 00:23:53,845
Then solve the exponent equation, and add the products. The next video makes formal power series exact: sums, products, inverses and composition.
